findings.body:
In the stream type handling at line 1319, a new `samples` variable is declared which shadows the outer `samples` variable declared at line 1214. This causes the sampling logic for listpacks to use the inner `samples` variable, but the outer `samples` variable (which controls the loop) is never updated. This can lead to incorrect memory estimation for streams.

The outer `samples` variable at line 1214 is initialized to 0 and used in the loop condition `while(samples < sample_size)`, but the inner `samples = 0` at line 1319 shadows it, so the outer loop control is broken.

::code{file="src/object.c" lines="1214-1220"}
    size_t elesize = 0, elecount = 0, samples = 0;

    /* All kv-objects has at least kvobj header and embedded key */
    size_t asize = malloc_usable_size((void *)o);

::code{file="src/object.c" lines="1315-1340"}
        raxIterator ri;
        raxStart(&ri,s->rax);
        raxSeek(&ri,"^",NULL,0);
        size_t lpsize = 0;
        size_t samples = 0;  // <-- SHADOWING BUG: shadows outer samples
        while(samples < sample_size && raxNext(&ri)) {
            unsigned char *lp = ri.data;
            /* Use the allocated size, since we overprovision the node initially. */
            lpsize += zmalloc_size(lp);
            samples++;
        }

The fix is to either:
1. Remove the `size_t` declaration on line 1319 to reuse the outer variable
2. Use a different variable name like `lp_samples` for the listpack sampling count

The same pattern occurs at lines 1256 and 1302 where `samples` is used with the outer scope correctly.
